from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "7. False Apostles"),
("p", "Claim: the New Testament warns of men who claimed the apostolic title and were not what they claimed. The warnings show that the title was tested, that the test was the gospel preached and the works wrought, and that no passage answers the danger by naming a successor to the apostles. The volume reads the three passages that speak of false apostles and reports what each says."),
("sub", "7.1 At Corinth"),
("p", "Paul warns the Corinthians of men who come with another message: “For if he that cometh preacheth another Jesus, whom we have not preached, or if ye receive another spirit, which ye have not received, or another gospel, which ye have not accepted, ye might well bear with him” (2 Corinthians 11:4). He then names what they are: “For such are false apostles, deceitful workers, transforming themselves into the apostles of Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:13)."),
("p", "The passage gives the test. The men preached another Jesus, another spirit and another gospel, and the Corinthians were told what had been preached to them and what they had received. The test is the content of what was preached. Paul adds that the disguise is no marvel: “for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light. Therefore it is no great thing if his ministers also be transformed as the ministers of righteousness; whose end shall be according to their works” (2 Corinthians 11:14, 15)."),
("tbl", "False apostles at Corinth.", [
    ("2 Corinthians 11:4", T("2 Cor 11:4"), "Another Jesus, another spirit, another gospel."),
    ("2 Corinthians 11:13–15", T("2 Cor 11:13-15"), "False apostles, deceitful workers, transforming themselves into the apostles of Christ."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "7.2 In Galatia"),
("p", "The same test stands in the opening of Galatians: “But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel unto you than that which we have preached unto you, let him be accursed. As we said before, so say I now again, If any man preach any other gospel unto you than that ye have received, let him be accursed” (Galatians 1:8, 9). The curse falls on the message and not on the title. It falls on Paul himself if he preaches another gospel, and on an angel from heaven. The standard is the gospel that was preached and received, and it stands above the one who brings it."),
("sub", "7.3 At Ephesus in the Revelation"),
("p", "The Lord speaks in the Revelation to the angel of the church at Ephesus: “I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience, and how thou canst not bear them which are evil: and thou hast tried them which say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars” (Revelation 2:2). The verse is read as written. The church tried those who said they were apostles, and found them liars. The Revelation is addressed to the servants of Jesus Christ in the setting its first verses name, and the volume does not carry the passage to the Body. It reports that, in the setting of the Revelation, men claimed the title and were tried."),
("sub", "7.4 What the passages show"),
("p", "Three things stand out. The first is that the title was claimed. A claim to be an apostle could be made falsely, and a church could be misled by it. The second is that the test was applied by the church: Corinth was told what to hold, and Ephesus tried the men. The third is that no passage answers the danger by pointing to a line of successors. Paul answers by pointing back to what he had preached and to the signs wrought among the Corinthians. The Lord, in the Revelation, commends the church for trying the claim."),
("p", "The volume draws one limit from this. If the office continued in a line of men appointed by the apostles, the natural answer to false claimants would be to point to the line. The passages do not do so. They point to the gospel preached, to the works wrought and to the Lord’s own word."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Men claimed the apostolic title falsely at Corinth, and the claim was tested by the gospel preached.",
    "Galatians 1:8, 9 puts the gospel above Paul himself and above an angel.",
    "In Revelation 2:2 the church at Ephesus tried those who said they were apostles and found them liars.",
    "No passage answers the danger by naming a successor.",
  ]),
]
